<PageTitle>Tenants &amp; roles — Vaultex</PageTitle>

<div class="docs-eyebrow">Concepts</div>
<h1>Tenants &amp; roles</h1>
<p class="docs-lead">
    Vaultex is multi-tenant from the ground up. Every user, role, and policy belongs to exactly one
    tenant, and tenants never see each other's data. This page explains how to model your customers
    and what role choices make sense.
</p>

<h2 id="model">The model</h2>
<ul>
    <li>A <strong>Vaultex deployment</strong> hosts many tenants.</li>
    <li>A <strong>tenant</strong> is one customer or environment. It owns users, roles, policies, audit logs.</li>
    <li>A <strong>user</strong> belongs to exactly one tenant.</li>
    <li>A <strong>role</strong> is a name like <code>Admin</code> or <code>Viewer</code>, scoped to its tenant.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="tid">How tenant context flows</h2>
<p>
    Vaultex stamps every token with a <code>tid</code> claim. Your app reads it and applies it as a
    filter on every query. Tenants never collide because your data layer enforces the boundary.
</p>

<h2 id="builtin-roles">Built-in roles</h2>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>What it can do</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>PlatformAdmin</code></td><td>Manage all tenants. Assigned by you, the operator. Not a tenant role.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Admin</code></td><td>Full control inside one tenant: users, roles, policies.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Manager</code></td><td>Manage users inside one tenant. Cannot edit roles or policies.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Viewer</code></td><td>Read-only access. The default for new sign-ups.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="custom-roles">Custom roles</h2>
<p>
    Add your own roles in <strong>Admin → Roles</strong>. They show up as <code>role</code> claims
    in tokens, and you can use them in your app's <code>[Authorize(Roles = "...")]</code> checks.
</p>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--info">
    <span class="docs-callout-icon">
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor"><path d="M11 7h2v2h-2zm0 4h2v6h-2zm1-9C6.48 2 2 6.48 2 12s4.48 10 10 10 10-4.48 10-10S17.52 2 12 2z"/></svg>
    </span>
    <div class="docs-callout-body">
        <div class="docs-callout-title">Roles vs policies</div>
        <p>Roles are coarse-grained ("manager"). Policies are fine-grained ("user X can edit invoice Y"). Use roles for org structure, policies for resource-level access.</p>
    </div>
</div>

